      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. I understand you've confirmed it. But 6,400 mg/day of ibuprofen is twice the maximum daily dose, and I know of no adult indication for it. It would put the patient at serious risk of GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events, and it wouldn't give more pain relief. Pharmacy verification is a backstop, and I don't want to rely on it for a dose I believe is unsafe.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- Ibuprofen 800 mg PO four times daily (3,200 mg/day, the maximum)\n- Ibuprofen 800 mg PO three times daily (2,400 mg/day)\n- Ibuprofen 400 mg PO four times daily (1,600 mg/day), if the intended total was 1,600 mg/day\n- A multimodal regimen, such as scheduled acetaminophen plus ibuprofen at a standard dose, if the goal is stronger analgesia\n\nIf there is a specific clinical reason for the higher dose, such as a protocol or a consult recommendation, tell me and we can look at it again. You can also talk to pharmacy directly about it."
      }
